    { h: 'Recruitment and the all or none law', html: `
<p>A <b>motor unit</b> is one motor neurone and all the muscle fibres it supplies. All fibres in a motor unit are the same type.</p>
<p>The <b>all or none law</b>: when a motor neurone is stimulated above its threshold, <b>all</b> the fibres in that motor unit contract fully; below the threshold, <b>none</b> contract. Force is graded by recruiting <b>more</b> motor units (and larger ones) and by stimulating them more often.</p>
<p>Fibres are recruited in order as intensity rises: <b>type I</b> at low intensity → <b>type IIa</b> at moderate–high intensity → <b>type IIx</b> at near-maximal effort.</p>
<div class="box why"><b class="lbl">Genetics and training</b><p>The proportion of fibre types is mainly genetic. Training improves the properties of fibres — e.g. endurance training makes type IIa fibres more fatigue resistant; power training increases the size of type II fibres.</p></div>` }
